In this lesson, we'll look at how to use a line of best fit to make predictions, and where it can lead you astray. First up, interpolation versus extrapolation. If you're predicting a y value for an x that sits right between your data points, that's interpolation, and it's usually pretty trustworthy. But if you try to predict outside the range you measured, that's extrapolation, and the linear pattern you've spotted might not hold out there. The further you go, the riskier it gets. Next, when a line just isn't appropriate. A line of best fit only makes sense when the relationship is roughly straight. If your scatterplot clearly curves, like a U-shape or something growing faster and faster, forcing a straight line through it will mislead you. Better to fit a curve, or break the data into straight sections. Then there's communicating uncertainty. Your predicted y is just an estimate. Real points scatter around the line, so the true value will probably be close, but not exactly equal to your prediction. Finally, residuals. A residual is just how far a point sits above or below the line, positive if it's above, negative if below. Plotting those against x is a great sanity check. Random scatter, good fit. Obvious pattern, rethink it. Now let's see it in action.